    "q-122353": {
      "dom_id": "step-q122353",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14228914/assets/77242f149dc99e0641e6e095d02aeeb1fffdf764ee664cfcc02e659f9d0e03b0.png)\n\nGiven the triangle above, find the length of $\\overset{―}{AC}$ rounded to one decimal place.",
      "worked_solution": "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the adjacent leg $\\overset{―}{AB}$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{AC}$ hypotenuse to the given angle $∠A.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse is *cosine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(m∠C) & =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({22}^{∘}) & =\\frac{7}{AC} \\\\ AC\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({22}^{∘}) & =7 \\\\ AC & =\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({22}^{∘})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({22}^{∘})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{(0.927\\,18…)} \\\\  & =7.549\\,74… \\\\  & ≈7.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.",
